{
  "id": "Compound::DB00880 - Gene::760",
  "verbalization": "Chlorothiazide, a thiazide diuretic, is consistently linked to the binding of calcium (Ca²⁺) through multiple pathways in the dataset. The evidence suggests that Chlorothiazide is part of a broader class of thiazide derivatives, which all exhibit a common mechanism of action involving the inhibition of sodium-potassium ATPase and subsequent effects on calcium homeostasis. This is supported by the binding of various thiazide analogs, such as Hydrochlorothiazide and Methyclothiazide, to Ca²⁺, indicating a shared biological function. The lowest common ancestors across these relationships point to the thiazide class, reinforcing the notion that these compounds act through a common mechanism. The integrated evidence suggests that Chlorothiazide, through its inclusion in thiazide derivatives, binds Ca²⁺ in a manner consistent with the pharmacological profile of thiazide diuretics, which is likely mediated by a direct interaction with calcium ions. This mechanism is further supported by the presence of multiple thiazide analogs that bind Ca²⁺, suggesting a general biological role in calcium regulation.",
